	doi = {10.1109/ISCAS66217.2026.11562990},
	abstract = {To overcome the challenges of high latency and low adaptability faced by conventional Reinforcement Learning (RL) for dexterous hand control, this paper presents a neuromorphic ASIC design that integrates Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) with on-chip learning. The proposed neuromorphic controller unifies offline SNN-RL training with online Reward-modulated Spike-Timing Dependent Plasticity (R-STDP) adaptation, enabling real-time on-chip weight updates without backpropagation. Hardware-efficient designs, such as sparsity-aware zero-skipping and Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) states lookup-table optimization, significantly reduce the number of operations and memory access. The proposed design is simulated under 40-nm CMOS technology, occupying a core area of 1.58 mm2 and achieving 559 Hz control frequency at 2.3 mW power. Experiment results demonstrate the controller can provide sub-millisecond latency control, rapid adaptation to new task, and about 100× training-time reduction compared with GPU-based implementations.},
