	abstract = {On-chip learning is the most advantageous feature of spiking neural networks (SNNs) that allows realizing lowpower artificial intelligence (AI) systems, especially for the edge computing technique and edge AI devices. Although there is still no principal learning method for SNNs, the spike timingdependent plasticity (STDP) learning rule is the most popular. Most implementations of the method predominantly defined synapses as conductors. This approach results in common on-chip training designs for digital systems that typically require finite state machines (FSMs) and look-up tables (LUTs), which evaluate and update the values of all neurons and synapses within a neural network. This combination limits the responding time of an SNN. An auto-plasticity synapse, which defines plasticity based on spike-time delay and independently controls this property, offers a solution to address this limitation. In this work, we proposed a 4-layer fully-connected SNN that uses 96 auto-plasticity synapses to validate this new type of synapse. This network synthesized into a {\textbackslash}mathbf2 8-{\textbackslash}mathbfn m field-programmable gate array (FPGA) Cyclone V with just 14.5 \% of resources, allowing the making of an edge AI system on a chip (SoC). The SNN was trained unsupervised, totally on-chip, and could classify the 3-class Irish data set with accuracy up to 90 \%.},
