	doi = {10.1109/TVLSI.2026.3696284},
	abstract = {Neuromorphic processors deploying spiking neural networks (SNNs) hold great potential for edge intelligence applications. However, achieving high accuracy, high throughput, and highly energy-efficient on-chip learning under limited energy and resource budgets remains a critical challenge. To address this, we propose a tiny neuromorphic architecture to support a fully spike-based backpropagation learning algorithm tailored for a lightweight two-layer SNN aiming at edge scenarios. To enhance processing efficiency, this architecture tightly couples control and data flows, and features fully spike-based inference and learning. Furthermore, we propose a structured weight mapping scheme that satisfies different parallel memory access patterns during forward and backward passes, and introduce a group-based event-driven paradigm to exploit spike sparsity. A prototype was implemented on a very-low-cost Xilinx Zynq-7010 FPGA device. It achieved high on-chip learning accuracies comparable to state-of-the-art works across multiple benchmark datasets, while delivering a throughput of 2269/9082 frames/s and an energy efficiency of 56/ 14 {\textbackslash}mu J/image for learning/inferring MNIST images, which outperforms existing approaches and shows its potential for wide deployments in extreme edge intelligence scenarios.},
