	doi = {10.1109/DECoN67170.2025.11447677},
	abstract = {The development of neuromorphic computing and the RISC-V-spiking neural network (SNN) accelerators has become an important source of energy-efficient edge intelligence. Recent designs, including SpikeStream and FeNN, achieved up to 4.4x speedup and 3.5x energy due to vectorized sparse processing and RV-SCNN and RISCVuzz achieved 90 \% of inference-cycle improvement and architecture-level fuzzing to improve hardware reliability. At the same time, fuzzing-based designs such as MABFuzz and ChatFuzz demonstrated higher vulnerability detection by 308 x and 75 coverage of 52 min and guaranteed reliable neuromorphic execution. Such fundamental systems as Loihi, SpiNNaker succeeded in tens-to-hundreds-fold energy reductions, establishing benchmarks to on-chip learning. Even with such innovations, it is still very difficult to combine fault detection, side-channel resistance, and adaptive energy control into single unified RISC-V systems. The paper suggests a Hybrid Adaptive Neuromorphic-RISC-V (HAN-RV) architecture which is a combination of spike-based workload profiling, dynamic voltage scaling, and bandit-based instruction fuzzing. Initial analysis shows that there is a possibility of 6 times throughput increase and power reduction of up to 80 percent over the state-of-the-art vectorized SNN cores. HAN-RV bridges the efficiency to neuromorphic inference and the security to verified and secure fuzzing strategies through the combination of learning-based optimization and the verified secure fuzzing strategies to offer a scalable roadmap of the next generation intelligent edge system.},
